DECUS '95/WASHINGTON, D.C. TO TRANSMIT SESSIONS OVER THE INTERNET

Live Worldwide Transmissions To Utilize The Internet Multicast Backbone (MBone)

Shrewsbury, MA, March 30, 1995 - The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital
Equipment Computer Users Society, today announced they will utilize the
Internet Multicast Backbone (MBone) protocol and transmission process to
multicast sessions from DECUS '95/Washington, D.C., the premier seminars,
symposium, and trade show event focused on the Digital marketplace.

The pilot program will showcase just a few of the many timely and
beneficial offerings presented at DECUS '95/Washington, D.C. via a live,
worldwide, audio/video feed.

Those with access to MBone technology will be able to view live
presentations from speakers including Internet pioneers Tim Berners-Lee
and Marc Andreessen.

Berners-Lee, who will present "The World Wide Web" keynote, was part of
the team which invented the World Wide Web.  With the number of Web
applications and rate of usage exploding, Berners-Lee is expected to
explain how he sees his invention changing in the coming years.

Andreessen, developer of the Mosaic browser, and vice president of
technology for Netscape Communications, literally brought the Web out of
the laboratory and into the mainstream.  During his session, "In the
Internet Fastlane", Andreessen will describe the next leap forward in
browsers and how we will be using the Web tomorrow.

Other featured sessions expected to be transmitted over the Internet
include the event's "Keynote Address" delivered by Enrico Pesatori, vice
president and general manager of Digital Equipment Corporation's Computer
Systems Division; and "Industry Trends and Futures" a focus on the
technical trends in the networks management arena, from networking and
security expert, executive vice president of Network-1, Dr. Bill Hancock.

Transmissions are expected to be multicast Monday, May 8 at 8:30 A.M.;
and Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, May 9 - 11, at 8:00 A.M. Eastern
Time.

More information on the MBone multicast from DECUS  95/Washington, D.C.
will be posted, as it becomes available, on the MBone page of the DECUS
Web server accessible via:

	http://www.decus.org/decus/events/dc/mbone.html/

MBone transmissions can be viewed through most computers running the UNIX
operating system, utilizing a network interface designed to receive
multicasts.  A set of tool applications, available from various FTP sites
on the Internet, are also required.  More information on the MBone can be
acquired online via:

	http://www.eit.com/techinfo/mbone/mbone.html/
	http://www.research.att.com/mbone-faq.html/
	ftp://genome-ftp.stanford.edu/pub/mbone/mbone-connect/
	ftp://venera.isi.edu/mbone/faq.txt/

Addressing the critical issues of importance to today's Information
Technology professionals, DECUS '95/Washington, D.C., May 6-11, 1995 at
the Washington Convention Center and Renaissance Hotel, will provide the
real-world solutions necessary to optimize today's distributed computing
enterprises.

More information on DECUS '95/Washington, D.C. is available by contacting
DECUS Customer Service at 1-800-DECUS55.  Outside the U.S. and Canada
call 1-508-841-7800.  Information is also available via: World Wide Web:
http//www.decus.org/, Anonymous FTP: ftp.decus.org, and E-mail:
mailserv@decus.org (send message HELP).

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society,
is an Association of Information Technology professionals interested in
the products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation
and related vendors.  The Association's purpose is to promote the
unimpeded exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members
and their organizations to be more successful.  The Chapter provides each
member with means to enhance their professional development, forums for
technical training, mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information,
advocacy programs, and opportunities for informal disclosure and
interaction with professional colleagues of like interests.

