Frame To Port To AlphaGeneration Workstations and Servers Running Digital UNIX

San Jose, Calif., March 27, 1995 - Frame Technology Corporation
announced its intention to port FrameMaker, FrameViewer, and Frame
Developer's Kit, release 5 products to AlphaGeneration workstations and
servers running Digital's UNIX operating system.

Frame release 5 products will be available for Digital UNIX in Q4 1995.
In the interim, Frame will begin offering its Version 4 products running on
Digital UNIX in early May.  Files created on the Digital Alpha workstations
are fully compatible with FrameMaker files created on Macintosh, Windows
and other UNIX platforms.

"Our commitment to support Digital's Alpha users is integral to Frame's
multiplatform strategy," stated Bill Pieser, vice president of marketing at
Frame Technology.  "Digital's workstations are an excellent fit for the
power and flexibility of Frame's products.  We are especially excited about
taking advantage of the 64-bit application environment available on Digital
workstations."

Frame offers software products and tools for the complete document
lifecycle, from creation and authoring, revisions and editing, tracking,
and retrieval to electronic distribution and viewing.

"This commitment from Frame illustrates the growing partnership between
Frame and Digital," stated Duncan Anderson, director of Digital's Software
Technology Group.  "Digital is delighted to extend our partnership with
Frame by offering leading document-based information solutions for our
mutual customers."

FrameMaker software combines word processing, page layout, graphics,
equations, tables and book-building tools for creating a wide range of
publications, from books and technical manuals to reports, newsletters
and presentations.

FrameViewer software, Frame's leading-edge viewing technology, allows
electronic, online distribution of documents.  It offers significant cost
savings over paper-based distribution of documents, improves access to
information through hypertext navigation, and ensures organizations have
up-to-date, online information.

The Frame Developer's Kit (FDK) is targeted at end-users, ISV developers,
value-added resellers, and systems integrators who want to create custom
applications around Frame's products.

The Digital UNIX operating system is the most standards-compliant
implementation of UNIX on the market. AlphaGeneration workstations and
servers won several awards in AIM Technology's 1994 Hot Iron Awards for
UNIX computer systems, demonstrating the many advantages of Digital's UNIX
based Alpha Systems.

