AlphaServer 8400 Delivers Mainframe Performance for Less Cost

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Delivers mainframe performance at one-tenth the cost

o  Very fast transaction processing

o  Large and fast data warehouses with Very Large Memory and Very Fast Data
   Access

o  World's fastest sort, new porting tools, and new high speed printer

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The AlphaServer 8400 exceeds mainframe performance at one-tenth the cost of
a traditional mainframe. With 6 processors and 10 GB of memory, the
AlphaServer 8400 is approximately $1.3 million, whereas a comparably
configured ES/9000 is priced at $16.8 million. Its 14 GB Very Large Memory
is significant to compute intensive and database intensive applications.
This system uses high speed disk stripped channels that achieve more usable
I/O than its mainframe counterparts, significantly outperforming even
mainframe alternatives from HP and Sun.

The move off the mainframe is increasing. Competitive pressures, the move
towards client/server computing, and shrinking budgets are fueling the
trend. The mainframe environment is particularly impacted because of the
lack of flexibility caused by rigid hierarchical architectures and lack of
user satisfaction associated with the mainframe.

Over 80 percent of all software code in the world is written on mainframes
in COBOL. Much of this code was developed in-house over the past 30 years,
making it costly to maintain and support. In-house user-developed solutions
are in need of replacement. The AlphaServer 8400, along with third party
partner applications, is an ideal downsizing solution. Popular mainframe
applications that could be move onto the AlphaServer 8400 include:

o  CICS (popular transaction processing monitor)
o  DB2, IMS, and other IBM database installations
o  SAS (statistical analysis and management reporting)
o  D&B Millennium (financial software)
o  IBI FOCUS (management reporting)
o  Syncsort SORTing installations
o  Mapper (popular 4GL) for UNISYS users
o  IBM PROFS (office automation) installations
o  And many more

Mainframe Solution Options

To downsize, applications are being refaced, rehosted, replaced,
rearchitected, and re-engineered. These options serve as the basis for
classifying mainframe migration activities and opportunities.

o  Reface -- A new graphical user interface front end is placed between a PC
   user and an existing system. Examples include ACCESSWORKS DB Integrator
   with other popular Windows based front end products.

o  Rehost  -- This popular strategy moves an existing application from a
   mainframe to a lower cost alternative. Some of the popular solutions that
   run on the mainframe are also available on Alpha systems, including SAS,
   FOCUS, D&B Millennium, CICS, and VIStp software.

o  Replace -- This strategy migrates from one application solution to
   another, typically to a leading third-party package. Some leading
   candidates are: SAP, PeopleSoft, Oracle Financials, D&B Millennium,
   SmartStream, Ross Financials, Concom's Control:Manufacturing, and CA's
   MANMAN. Another replacement example is PROFS to ALL-IN-1 and TeamLinks
   software or another Windows based solution.

o  Rearchitect -- This option converts, rewrites, or moves software to a
   client/server environment. Examples include transaction processing, sort,
   print, and data warehouse. All of the database vendors offer data
   warehouse solutions, as well as decision support solutions such as FOCUS,
   SAS, and Software AG's NATURAL. More specialized data warehouse tools
   include Prism, Red Brick, HOPS, InfoHarvester, and Omnidex.

o  Re-engineer -- This option is a business process redesign. Business
   process redesign via consulting services is the art of designing a new
   way of doing business. Re-engineering is done at a level higher than
   computer systems.

Transaction Processing Environments

Transaction processing applications, among significant considerations,
require speed. The combination of Very Large Memory, Very Fast Data Access,
Very Fast Transaction Processing, and raw performance give the AlphaServer
8400 significant advantages. Digital has ported and is now announcing IBM's
RS6000 CICS on Alpha systems and provides the ability to use and maintain
IBM's environment to leverage the installed CICS environment and skillset.
Many users prefer rehosting CICS because it provides the least costly
approach to migrating applications to the Alpha platform as well as the
ability to interoperate with other CICS applications on the mainframe and
other platforms. Additionally, VIS/TP from VISystems provides the ability to
migrate CICS to Digital UNIX platforms, maintaining the CICS environment.
(See the "CICS for Digital UNIX" article in this issue.)

Another new product, Conveyor, from Infosoft, provides the ability to
convert diverse mainframe environments (including CICS) to either Digital
UNIX or OpenVMS platforms. However, there is no TP monitor used by the
converted code, nor will it include Task/Path control. Conveyor appeals to
users who want to get rid of CICS code or have lower performance
requirements.

Sort and Print

Mainframes spend considerable amounts of time sorting. Syncsort is used by
over 60 percent of IBM's users. Digital is now announcing new performance
breakthroughs using Syncsort, as sorts are as I/O intensive as any job. (See
the Price/Performance section of this article for more details.) Printing is
an important aspect of mainframe output. For applications requiring high
speed print environments, offloading the print activities can delay the
costs of an expensive mainframe upgrade. At the same time new competitive
functionality can be added with data driven color, all at a lower cost of
operation.

A new high speed Xerox data driven printer will soon be available on OpenVMS
Alpha platforms. It will print from 50 to 135 cut sheet pages per minute
with print volumes in excess of 2 million pages per month. Xerox printers
will connect to Alpha systems via a dedicated SCSI channel to a shared disk
subsystem. Data will be transferred to the shared disk subsystem at SCSI
speeds and then will be removed at the same speed and printed via the Xerox
printer. This will be the only solution for Digital systems that print at
engine speed. Xerox printers will provide the solutions users are looking
for as they migrate from their mainframes to distributed client/server
computing.

Price/Performance

Price and performance are of critical importance to mainframe users. IBM
mainframe users will be very interested to hear about the most popular
mainframe sorting software, Syncsort. The Syncsort benchmark is the world's
fastest -- it sorted 1 GB in less than five minutes, half the time it takes
a high end traditional mainframe. The benchmark covers reading data from
disk, performing the sort, and writing the sorted data back to disk.

Another widely visible benchmark is the Oracle Very Large Data Base
benchmark using 10 GB of memory, yielding unprecedented results in a 800
percent performance improvement than the same comparable database running in
only a 2 GB memory space.

The AlphaServer 8400 is a highly balanced server capable of up to 14 GB of
memory, up to 12-way symmetric multiprocessing, with over 10 TB (terebytes)
of data storage, high speed disk stripped I/O channels, and a 2 GB internal
switch. Compute intensive and Very Large Data Base applications excel in
this environment. The Very Large Memory capacity allows for more data to be
memory resident. Having a Very Large Memory area dramatically increases the
chances the data is already in memory. Because memory speeds are 500,000
times faster than disk seek and access speeds, overall performance is
significantly enhanced.

BENEFITS

Traditional mainframes have always been expensive and continue to be so.
With the advent of the AlphaServer 8400, mainframe migration solutions are
now affordable. One example is that of a high-end data warehouse. Prior to
the AlphaServer 8400, only a high-end traditional mainframe or specialized
server would be considered because of its superior I/O and data access
capabilities. The AlphaServer 8400 offers superior data access and
performance. This is but one example, for TP compute intensive, and other
database intensive applications will also benefit. Very large memory
benefits can be seen in any of three dimensions:
1) Dramatic increases in the number of simultaneous users; or
2) Response time improves significantly with the same number of users; or
3) Dramatic increases in the ability to process complex queries without loss
   in response times. Compute bound applications, data warehousing and
   decision support software ring up huge benefits with the AlphaServer 8400
   Very Large Memory capacity.

Very Fast Data Access and Very Fast Transaction Processing are also dramatic
as either the data and/or the database meta data (data pointers to the
actual data) can now reside in memory and be accessed at memory speeds.  If
the data is on disk this is where I/O speed and other internals become
incredibly important.

