Upgrading to AlphaServer 8200 and 8400 Systems

o  System, cluster, and application upgrades available

o  New higher performance Alpha upgrade paths

o  Significantly lower operating and maintenance costs

DESCRIPTION

System Upgrades

Now is a good time to upgrade older, more costly VAX and MIPS systems to
newer, more powerful and less expensive platforms. Older VAX systems for
example can be consolidated into a reduced number of high reliability, high
performance Alpha systems -- significantly lowering operating and
maintenance costs. Often, the savings in maintenance alone is greater than
the monthly cost associated with leasing or purchasing a new system.

The cost of operations differential between the older installed systems and
newer VAX and Alpha systems is dramatic as illustrated below:

			      Annual Cost
System			      of Operations	      % Savings (1)

New AlphaServer 8400 5/300    $5,514 (2)	      --
VAX 8350		      $7,934		      30%
VAX 8550		      $22,237		      75%
VAX 8810		      $39,359		      86%

(1) Savings achieved are in power, floorspace, and maintenance costs.
(2) Maintenance costs for the first year are included in system purchase
    price.

The new AlphaServer 8400 systems offer a new higher performance upgrade path
for users who have enterprise class servers installed, such as VAX 6000,
8000, 9000, and 10,000; and need the performance, I/O, and expandability
that the AlphaServer 8400 offers. For example, the performance of a
uniprocessor AlphaServer 8200 or 8400 is five to six times the performance
of a VAX 7610 for compute intensive applications, and three to four times
the commercial performance. It also offers five times the I/O of a VAX or
DEC 7000 system. The AlphaServer 8400 is also an ideal upgrade path for VAX
and DEC 7000 users due to its expandability and the investment protection it
offers. Both VAX and DEC 7000 systems can be easily upgraded to the
AlphaServer 8400, preserving the cabinetry, power, and all I/O investments.

For users with VAX and Alpha departmental systems, the new top-of-the-line
AlphaServer 8200 server offers an ideal upgrade solution. With up to six
processors, this system offers small enterprises and large departments the
most powerful, expandable, and reliable server at the best
price/performance.

Upgrading to a new AlphaServer 8200 or 8400 system has never been easier.
Digital offers the highest level of investment protection--because the same
data, applications, networks, clusters and peripherals can be used. With
Digital's ADVANTAGE-UPGRADE program, users receive a trade-in on their
existing system and can save up to 75 percent on Digital software licenses
with the purchase of a system upgrade. The program also includes a free
60-day transition period that allows users to move their VAX software to the
new Alpha system easily. (Upgrades may be handled differently in Europe;
please contact your local Digital sales or business partner office for
assistance.)

OpenVMS Cluster Upgrades

The ability to consolidate OpenVMS Clusters into a reduced number of highly
reliable, high performance AlphaServer 8400 or 8200 systems offers numerous
advantages. Adding Alpha systems to an existing OpenVMS VAX Cluster provides
the ability to increase system capacity and smoothly migrate existing VAX
applications over time.

Users continue to place increased demands on their OpenVMS Cluster
environments in terms of numbers of users supported, amount of storage
required, and more performance-demanding applications. Couple this with
decreasing IT budgets and datacenter managers are facing enormous pressure
to meet management and user needs at much lower costs.

The ability to provide users with more reliable and faster solutions
translates to higher user satisfaction. The ability to provide management
with reduced spending and improved customer satisfaction translates into
saved jobs. Also, datacenter floor space is costly. Consolidating several
machines in an OpenVMS Cluster saves space, electricity, maintenance, and
cooling costs.

Users also have the option of adding an AlphaServer system to an existing
OpenVMS Cluster to increase capacity and off-load applications that require
additional processing power. This approach also aids in the migration from
VAX to Alpha systems since all applications do not have to be moved at once.

The new AlphaServer 8400 and 8200 systems, Digital's highest performing
servers, can be easily integrated with existing OpenVMS Clusters. These new
servers are particularly suited to environments where users have compute
intensive, memory resident applications such as scientific and technical
programs or databases. Users are now able to configure their OpenVMS
Clusters in geographically distant locations using T3 or FDDI connections.

AlphaServer 8200 and 8400 systems both support DSSI clusters. The
AlphaServer 8200 supports DSSI clustering using the EISA adapter called the
KFESB. Today, OpenVMS software supports two of these adapters per system.
The AlphaServer 8400 can use either an EISA adapter (KFESB), or the KFMSB,
which is the XMI to DSSI adapter.

For CI-based datacenter cluster configurations, the AlphaServer 8400
supports up to 10 CIXCD adapters per system. Because the AlphaServer 8200
does not have an XMI bus, it is currently not possible to configure a CI on
the AlphaServer 8200.

See the OpenVMS V6.2 SPD for further details.

ALL-IN-1 and Teamlinks Software Upgrades and Migration to Alpha Systems

User computing needs have changed. PC LANs have developed alongside VAX
based networks and users want to integrate them. At the same time, users are
under increased pressure to deliver more capability to their users at much
lower costs.

ALL-IN-1 Office Server V3.1, now available on Alpha systems, along with
TeamLinks Office for Windows and Macintosh users, provides the foundation
for building an office strategy that answers future needs.  ALL-IN-1 for
OpenVMS software, combined with the leadership price/performance of the new
generation of AlphaServer systems, provides more power, lower operating
costs, and a long-term future.

Together, ALL-IN-1 and TeamLinks software provides everything users need to
integrate PC applications for both Windows and Macintosh desktops with
existing ALL-IN-1 and PATHWORKS environments. Users will not only benefit
from client/server capabilities but will also reap the rewards of ALL-IN-1
software's powerful enterprisewide mail system, something LAN-based
solutions cannot deliver.

The new AlphaServer systems will support 6 to 10 times the number of
ALL-IN-1 users at a fraction of the cost of older VAX systems. Therefore,
users can replace multiple older VAX and OpenVMS clustered systems with
fewer AlphaServer systems. The new Alpha system could pay for itself in a
matter of months as a result of the savings in maintenance alone. In
addition, the newly announced TeamLinks Server Packages offer electronic
mail, VTX, conferencing, and calendar capabilities at a 40 percent lower
price than the sum of the individually-priced parts.

The aggressive price/performance of ALL-IN-1 software on the new AlphaServer
systems makes server-based solutions extremely cost competitive when
compared with LAN-based systems, and will not require users to re-write
applications or re-train users. In addition, TeamLinks software enables
users to move to a client/server style of computing easily and cost
effectively. It also offers a much more robust environment for
enterprisewide networks than LAN-based solutions, and enables users to
integrate popular desktop applications through a graphical user interface.

Oracle V6 to ORACLE7 Software on Alpha Platform Upgrade

Many users are looking for ways to accelerate performance, increase the
availability of their Oracle based applications and improve price/
performance for online transaction processing and decision support
applications. This required the full support of their IS vendors.

Running the latest release of the powerful ORACLE database management
system, ORACLE7 Release 7.1, on the OpenVMS operating system, users can take
advantage of Alpha performance and expanded capacity for the most demanding
applications. They also have access to Oracle's new Parallel Server
technology for splitting a database across multiple hardware systems.

Combining ORACLE7, OpenVMS, and Parallel Server software with Digital's
OpenVMS Cluster technology will enable users to benefit from:

o  High availability for mission-critical applications.
o  Centralized system management.
o  Disk optimization -- ORACLE7 software distributes the I/O workload to
   multiple disk services.
o  Streamlined performance -- ORACLE7 database's shared memory strategy
   reduces I/O by 70 to 90 percent.
o  Multiple application performance -- ORACLE7 software runs both online
   OLTP and decision support systems on the same data, without the loss of
   performance or accuracy.
o  Scalable performance -- Parallel cache management ensures the scalable
   performance of ORACLE7 software in loosely coupled or massively parallel
   environments.

OpenVMS V6.1 users have equivalent functionality on both VAX and Alpha
systems. OpenVMS software users can take advantage of the new generation of
very high performance, cost effective multi-processor AlphaServer systems.

The AlphaServer 8200 and 8400 provide leadership performance and
price/performance. Because of their power, large memory capacity, and
expandability, they are ideal for users with large Oracle databases who need
higher performance and want to take advantage of the higher reliability and
throughput of ORACLE7 software.

Rdb Software

Digital's Rdb database management system was recently acquired by Oracle,
which has committed to support and enhance the Rdb product. Users who had
been concerned about the product's long-term direction no longer have to
worry. Also, the performance of Rdb software on Alpha systems is outstanding
(see "ORACLE7 and Oracle Rdb Database Product Families Now Available from
Digital" article in the last issue of Digital's Customer Update for more
details).

