FullVideo Supreme Video Capture/Output for AlphaStation Systems

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Digital's state-of-the-art full motion video capture and video output PCI
   option

o  Independent of installed graphics subsystem, single slot PCI card, for
   unlimited graphics and multimedia flexibility

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

FullVideo Supreme, Digital's first video PCI board for AlphaStation
workstations, delivers full motion video input and output. It is an enabling
technology to add video capture and live playback to all applications across
industries, including engineering, education, science, manufacturing, and
healthcare. Horizontal applications, such as collaborative conferencing,
email, presentations, online help, and databases, significantly enhance
productivity when combined with video. This product is available initially
for Digital UNIX (formerly called DEC OSF/1) users.

Designed to take advantage of AlphaStation system performance, FullVideo
Supreme is coupled with software compression to capture and create
audio/video (AVI) files. Digital's patented AccuVideo rendering technology
for video playback provides remarkable quality on typical 8 bit graphics
subsystems.

FullVideo Supreme also takes advantage of the AlphaStation system's fast PCI
bus that can accommodate a large volume of data, supporting the requirements
of full motion video. FullVideo Supreme supports full size (NTSC 640 x 480)
and full motion (30 frames per second) capture. The option can be added to
any AlphaStation system, and used with all supported AlphaStation graphics
subsystems.

Through Multimedia Services V1.5 for Digital UNIX systems (SPD 48.92.05), an
application can incorporate the data types and features of both audio and
video, by using the capabilities of the FullVideo Supreme coupled with 16
bit stereo quality audio in AlphaStation workstations. In the near future,
FullVideo Supreme will be supported under Windows NT and OpenVMS systems.

Similar to the Sound and Motion J300 TURBOchannel option, the FullVideo
Supreme includes the following features:

o  Full-motion, full size video input in NTSC, PAL, and SECAM is provided in
   composite format:
   - 30 frames per second NTSC, 640 x 480.
   - 60 fields per second NTSC, 640 x 240.
   - 50 fields per second PAL, SECAM, 768 x 288.

o  NTSC or PAL video output is provided in composite format to record data
   from video input or graphics display to videotape, or display to a TV
   monitor:
   - NTSC is supported in full frame output.
   - PAL is supported in field output only.

o  Video can be captured and displayed on the workstation screen in the
   following modes:
   - YUV 4:2:2 or YCrCb 4:2:2.
   - 8 bit pseudo-color (through AccuVideo rendering).
   - 8 bit greyscale.
   - 24 bit true color (with software assistance).
   (FullVideo Supreme does not support direct RGB input.)

o  It is independent of an installed graphics subsystem (single slot PCI
   card), allowing complete flexibility in the breadth of Digital's graphics
   offerings, from 2D to high-end 3D. The frame-buffer free design helps
   integrate video into a standard X-based or Windows NT operating
   environment.

o  AccuVideo provides best-in-class video rendering (patent pending) in
   hardware, with superior perceptual image quality and flexibility with a
   choice in the number of output colors, brightness, contrast, saturation,
   and image sharpening.

o  Digital noise filtering in hardware not only improves the quality of the
   displayed image, but improves the compression and scaling of images as
   well.

o  Video scaling maps video to window size from icon to full screen size.
   Video windows can be sized dynamically.

o  FullVideo Supreme includes these connectors:
   - RCA composite video input.
   - RCA composite video output.

Multimedia Services V1.5 for Digital UNIX Systems

Multimedia Services V1.5 provides the runtime and application programming
interface to support the FullVideo Supreme and built-in audio on
AlphaStation platforms. The interface will be extended as needed to support
new hardware features in these and future multimedia products.

The required Multimedia Services runtime license required for the FullVideo
Supreme is included with all workstation packages and factory-installed,
built-to-order workstations.

Multimedia Services runtime environment includes audio and video capture and
playback applications to use the FullVideo Supreme and any of the supported
audio cards that may be in the Alpha workstations. Multimedia Services
Development Software includes an API, sample applications, and programmer's
manual.

Please see the "Multimedia Services for Digital UNIX V1.5" article in this
issue for a full description on the newest features and types of software
compression and decompression supported.

Performance Considerations

Performance of displaying live video through the FullVideo Supreme or
capturing video to disk is highly dependent on a number of system features.
For example, displaying live video on the monitor is highly dependent on the
speed of the PCI bus and the type of graphics card in the system. Please
refer to the "Graphics Performance Summary" for specific performance of the
XShPutImage call on the various graphics options. In general, all
AlphaStation workstations with the ZLXp-E1 graphics option can display at
least one 640 x 480 size image at 30 frames per second.

Capturing live video from either option to disk is highly dependent on the
speed of the I/O subsystem. Capturing uncompressed data puts a much greater
strain on a system compared to capturing compressed data. For example,
approximately 15 frames per second of 320 x 240 uncompressed video may be
captured to a RZ26 disk on a AlphaStation 250 4/266 system. Performance can
be greatly improved by using multiple disks in a stripe set.

Applications

FullVideo Supreme, combined with InSoft's Communique! (available soon) and a
desktop camera, is ideal for low cost LAN based video conferencing
solutions. Using InSoft's software with CellB compression and decompression,
two AlphaStation users can expect video conferencing at 20 to 30 frames per
second (320 x 240), depending on system performance.

AimTech's IconAuthor is an iconic flow chart authoring system that can be
used to graphically create interactive training and commercial kiosks.
Running on Digital UNIX and Windows NT systems, IconAuthor can be combined
with FullVideo Supreme on an AlphaStation system for a complete authoring
workstation.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

FullVideo Supreme will be available in April 1995. Multimedia Services for
Digital UNIX runtime software is required. The license is included with
every AlphaStation package and the software is factory installed. It is also
available on the Layered Products CD-ROM for Digital UNIX systems. For those
adding audio and video capabilities to applications, order the development
license, Multimedia Services Development Software for Digital UNIX systems.
(See the "Multimedia Services for Digital UNIX V1.5" article in this issue
for complete description and all licenses offered.) At a minimum, DEC OSF/1
V3.0 is required.

