Digital's UNIX Operating System Name Changes to Digital UNIX

HIGHLIGHTS

o  To reinforce the UNIX brand and better reflect the true nature of
   Digital's standard UNIX product, Digital has changed the name of DEC
   OSF/1 to Digital UNIX

o  The product remains the same; same documentation, same price, same
   everything -- only the name changed

o  While the name change is effective immediately, it will be formally
   implemented with the next release of the product, Digital UNIX V3.2C,
   which is scheduled to ship in August

DESCRIPTION

At a recent press conference supported by Digital and other leading UNIX
system vendors, X/Open Company Limited announced that as part of its
licensing program for the UNIX trademark, operating system products branded
as conformant to the Single UNIX Specification (formerly Spec 1170) will be
able to carry the X/Open UNIX brand.

Development of Digital's DEC OSF/1 UNIX operating system has closely tracked
the Single UNIX Specification. As a result, DEC OSF/1 V3.2 met the branding
criteria and received the UNIX brand in early March. In licensing the X/Open
UNIX trademark, Digital warrants that DEC OSF/1 conforms and will continue
to conform to the specification, assuring users of a specified level of
compatibility and maximum application portability between DEC OSF/1 and
other UNIX systems.

Digital believes that its conformance to this important specification will
reduce users' risk in selecting open systems and facilitate integration
across a range of products.

Digital publicly announced the new name on March 14 at the U.S. UniForum
tradeshow in Dallas, Texas. The change is effective immediately but will
formally take effect with the next release of the product, Digital UNIX
V3.2C, which is scheduled to ship this summer. At that time, product
packaging, media, documentation, and collateral will use the new name,
carrying a "formerly DEC OSF/1" note for one year. This will accommodate
applications whose literature references DEC OSF/1 software. As new versions
of these products are released and new collateral produced, all DEC OSF/1
references will be changed to the Digital UNIX name.

It is important to note that this is not a product change. Digital is simply
changing the name; everything else remains the same. For the first time,
because of the X/Open action, Digital has the right to call the product what
it really is, UNIX software.

Digital expects most major UNIX vendors to seek the UNIX brand; a small
number may also change their product names to reflect the brand.

