LSF V2.1 Load Sharing and Distributed Batch Software

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Enhanced batch features, giving users and administrators more flexibility
   and greater control over batch jobs

o  Support for batch interoperability with Cray NQS

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Digital and Platform Computing Corporation announce the availability of the
LSF V2.1 release. This version includes a number of new features requested
by LSF users, in addition to software fixes from LSF V2.0. Platform will
continue to aggressively add features to LSF in response to LSF user
requests in parallel to research and development efforts for the next major
release. For more information, please see the "LSF V2.0 Load Sharing and
Distributed Batch Software for UNIX Networks" article in the January 1995
Customer Update.

Some of the more important new features in LSF V2.1 are:

o  Interoperability with NQS -- Some LSF sites need to send batch jobs to
   remote hosts running NQS (such as Cray machines). LSF V2.1 supports
   interoperability with NQS. LSF users may have their batch jobs
   transparently sent to an NQS system to run and receive job results. Users
   may use any supported user interface, including lsbatch commands, lsNQS
   (the NQS commands), and xlsbatch, to submit, monitor, signal, and delete
   their batch jobs that are running in an NQS system.

o  Fairshare Scheduling within Batch Queues -- The conventional
   first-come-first-serve (FCFS) job scheduling would be unfair when some
   users submit many batch jobs at one time, because other user jobs
   submitted later must wait until all previously submitted jobs complete
   when there is resource contention.

   LSF V2.0 supports fairshare scheduling for a group of hosts called a host
   partition. LSF V2.1 expands on this to allow fairshare scheduling within
   a job queue. By configuring a queue with the fairshare scheduling policy,
   jobs submitted by various users to this queue may be scheduled in a
   non-FCFS fashion. In general, a job would have a higher execution
   priority if the job owner has more (configurable) shares, less running
   jobs, has used less CPU time, and the job has waited longer. If all the
   users are assigned the same shares, jobs in this queue will be scheduled
   in a round robin fashion.

o  Automatic Job Queue Selection -- In LSF V2.1, a batch job may be
   submitted without specifying a queue name. lsbatch automatically selects
   an appropriate queue for it, based on the job's resource limits and other
   submission specifications, such as the requested host(s), user's
   accessibility to a queue, queue status (closed or open), and the
   allowance of exclusive execution. Thus, a user does not have to worry
   about which queue to which the job should be submitted. The list of
   candidate queues and the order in which these candidate queues are
   considered in selection are configurable.

   To have more direct control of queue selection, a user may define the
   default queue using an environment variable LSB_DEFAULTQUEUE. The list of
   queues associated with this variable will be considered in turn and the
   first usable queue will be selected.

o  Migration of Non-Checkpointable Jobs -- In LSF V2.1, non-checkpointable
   jobs may be migrated from the current execution host to another host if
   they were submitted as rerunnable jobs. Automatic job migration may also
   apply to such jobs. The same configurable policies for checkpointable
   jobs will be applied to rerunnable jobs, except the jobs will be rerun
   from beginning rather than from the last checkpoint.

o  Aggressive Pre-emptive Scheduling of Batch Jobs -- In LSF V2.0, a job,
   even it it is suspended, holds a job slot for its execution host,
   potentially preventing a higher priority job from being dispatched to
   this host because the host job limit has been reached. This is to
   guarantee resources to dispatched jobs. But in some cases, it causes
   higher priority jobs to wait for long periods of time and leads to
   underutilization of the execution host.

   In LSF V2.1, a queue may be configured to enable aggressive pre-emptive
   scheduling. Jobs in a pre-emptive queue may preempt lower priority jobs.
   Higher priority jobs may still be dispatched to run even though the job
   limit of the host has been reached. Running jobs of lower priority would
   be suspended by the system in order to ensure that the number of running
   jobs (excluding suspended jobs) continues to be within the host's and
   user's job limits.

