LinkWorks Image

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Tools for scanning, distribution, and identification of images in a
   LinkWorks environment

o  High degree of automation allows high throughput

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

LinkWorks Image (SPD 54.95) is a set of application programs that allows a
pile of paper to be scanned, organized as image documents, and made
available at LinkWorks desktops as LinkWorks objects.

Adding image handling to LinkWorks software is easy -- users only need an
image editor. The challenge is to add image handling in environments with
high performance imaging, rather than for scanning a document periodically,
in environments where image plays a relatively minor role in the work. The
challenge is in environments where access to image documents provides the
basis for the work of the company or department. Such environments are often
characterized by very large volumes of new images daily. Examples are claims
processing in insurance companies, handling tax forms in tax offices, and
handling order forms in mail order companies.

The applications in LinkWorks Image are complete and ready to use. All the
applications run on a LinkWorks desktop. This allows LinkWorks software to
be used to provide the facilities for the procedural work. LinkWorks
workflow can be used, and mail or data can be stored in shared cabinets and
folders.

Many commercial environments have particular requirements, depending on the
nature of the business, the particular company, or national laws. In fact,
the main value of LinkWorks Image is not that it provides complete
applications. The real value is that the applications provide example
tooling that can be used to show how entering of image documents can be
realized with a minimum of user interaction. To allow this, the applications
are provided as Visual Basic sources.

LinkWorks Image consists of three applications:

o  SCANtool -- Scans a large number of pages with high performance and easy
   handling. With SCANtool, the batches are stored in batch trays (LinkWorks
   folders can only contain batches). The scan process can be stopped at any
   moment (e.g., to set another scanning setting or for a paper jam). It is
   then possible to resume the scan activity or start scanning the complete
   batch from the beginning. The scanning activity can be performed with a
   minimum of user interactions. In fact, the scan-station PC is normally
   only needed to start and stop the scan activities. It is possible to
   introduce new standard settings and change the destination of the scanned
   information.

   The scanned batch of pages is automatically assigned some attributes --
   identifier: name of the scan-station; creation date and time; and the
   paper type. These allow the source of the information to be identified
   later in the process, which can be needed, for example, to track and
   correct poor quality, or for legal reasons.

o  DISTRtool -- Organizes the output of SCANtool into batches and
   distributes them to departments. During the distribution process the
   quality of the scan process can be checked (quality control can be done
   at any step). If necessary, pages or whole batches can be re-scanned.

o  IDENTtool -- Organizes a batch into image documents, assigns attributes,
   and stores the documents in LinkWorks folders. When a batch arrives at a
   department, the pages are grouped into documents. In addition, there are
   functions to assign a document to a folder, to create new folders, and to
   initiate workflow on the newly created documents or folders. After
   processing by IDENTtool, the image documents are available in the
   LinkWorks environment for the applications that need them, such as
   Workflow.

BENEFITS

o  Enables high volume production processing of images in a LinkWorks
   environment

o  High degree of automation, with very little operator interaction
   required. However, it does allow operator intervention at many points
   (e.g., quality control).

o  Can be adapted to particular processes.

