Digital's Client/Server Infrastructure Roadmap and Services

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Digital's Client/Server Roadmap outlines the transition path for all
   users moving from host/terminal to client/server computing

o  The Building an Open Client/Server Infrastructure service helps users
   reduce the time required to choose and design client/server
   infrastructure components

o  Digital's Infrastructure Transition Planning service helps users define
   their End-Point Model, and outlines the most efficient process for
   planning, designing, and implementing the infrastructure

DESCRIPTION

Digital's client/server consulting strategy, the Client/Server Roadmap,
outlines the four basic stages for any user moving to client/server
computing, and maps Digital's services and capabilities to those stages.
The stages are:

o  Align Business and IT Strategies
o  Build a Client/Server Infrastructure
o  Integrate and Migrate Data and Applications
o  Manage and Operate the Client/Server Environment

To successfully transition to client/server, all of these stages are
critical.

Many users will be in multiple stages of the Client/Server Roadmap. For
example, users who are engaged in business process re-engineering (BPR) are
in the first stage of the Client/Server Roadmap -- Aligning Business and IT
Strategies. Efforts by users and IS to review and select applications, both
for legacy systems and new applications, fall into the third stage of the
Client/Server Roadmap -- Integrating and Migrating Data and Applications.

Digital's Client/Server Roadmap is the key to a consistently planned and
implemented client/server transition. Digital's experience, gained from
designing and implementing its own worldwide, three-tier client/server
environment, and from consulting with Fortune 1000 users, delivers proven
methodologies and strategies that allow users to save time and money.

The Roadmap leads users through each stage of their client/server
transition, outlining choices and alternate routes, with recommendations
tailored to different needs. It can help coordinate multiple efforts
currently under way within a user's organization so that they adhere to a
master plan. Also, if a user is working with another consulting company for
BPR, Digital can partner with that company to supply technology consulting
for infrastructure services, or management services.

(To obtain the PostScript file containing art for this aricle, type COPY
SALES::SNUFFY$:[SU_LIB]2704_CSRDMP.PS/LOG *.* at the prompt.)

According to Gartner Group's "Client Survey of Workload Migration (April
1994), "By year-end 1995, 97 percent of enterprises plan to migrate workload
off mainframes, primarily to gain the functionality of a distributed
solution." To ensure that these migrated applications and new client/server
applications can be used to their fullest extent, a client/server
infrastructure must be in place.

Gartner Group further states ("Staff, Architecture and Infrastructure in
Migration," May 1994): "Without the infrastructure plan, the staffing, the
technology to support both the AD (Application Development) and the
execution environment, durable and maintainable applications will be
difficult to deploy and, in addition, hidden (end-user borne) IS costs will
continue to escalate."

For users just beginning to plan their client/server infrastructures,
Digital offers two introductory-level infrastructure services, the Building
an Open Client/Server Infrastructure (BOCSI) and Infrastructure Transition
Planning (ITP) services. Ensuring that the proper infrastructure is in place
for supporting and managing both legacy systems and new client/server
systems falls under the second and fourth stages of the Roadmap -- Building
a Client/Server Infrastructure and Managing a Client/Server Environment
respectively.

BOCSI is a low-priced consulting engagement that can:

o  Be quickly purchased by users without several levels of management
   approval.
o  Jump-start users' infrastructure planning and implementation efforts.
o  Address the needs of medium to large enterprises.
o  Be delivered at a fixed price.

ITP is:

o  The logical follow-on service to BOCSI.
o  A more complete service for complex infrastructure transition needed at
   large enterprises.
o  A customized service that can accommodate unique user situations.
o  For IS departments with limited trained resources in client/server
   computing.
o  For IS departments with primarily IBM experience.
o  For companies looking to deploy client/server solutions on a worldwide
   basis.

BOCSI Service

The BOCSI service has three components:

o  An assessment of the user's current computing infrastructure.

o  A workshop that discusses the eight components comprising a client/server
   infrastructure and explains the dependencies and interrelationships among
   the components. The workshop focuses on relating the client/server
   infrastructure model to the user's environment, highlighting design and
   implementation considerations.

o  A report that summarizes the workshop findings and makes recommendations
   for the user's new infrastructure design and implementation plan,
   including the Infrastructure Planning Primer. This Primer outlines the
   steps necessary to develop a global infrastructure plan.

BOCSI is competitively priced, and designed to be flexible and customized to
the user's environment. Two packages are available, one with an eight-module
workshop and one with an five-module workshop.

ITP Service

Users implementing client/server applications need to define the vision or
"End-Point Model," and an overall plan for their client/server
infrastructure. These are critical; to achieve the benefits of client/server
computing, the client/server infrastructure must support anticipated usage
via applications and users.

Digital's ITP service helps users define their End-Point Model and outlines
the most efficient process for planning, designing, and implementing the
infrastructure.

The End-Point Model specifies the strategies, technologies, and standards
for implementing the different components of the infrastructure. Without
such a blueprint, large organizations spend thousands of dollars and still
end up with disparate infrastructures that do not support interoperability
among applications and data.

The second deliverable of ITP is the creation of a multi-year project plan
describing the various discrete tasks that must be completed to upgrade the
infrastructure, their sequence of execution, dependencies, desired
timelines, budgets, and business and technical constraints and risks. This
plan is continuously updated based on business and technology
considerations. Once prepared, such a plan can then be used to plan yearly
IT projects and budgets.

Digital's systems integration consultants use a proven methodology that
takes into account the user's current environment, application initiatives,
industry standards, and technology trends to develop a custom infrastructure
design optimized for business and technology strategies.

BENEFITS

BOCSI and ITP consulting services can provide worldwide support for the
user's global infrastructure planning, design, and implementation needs.

The BOCSI service provides:

o  An understanding of the key components of a client/server infrastructure,
   including their dependencies and relationships.
o  An understanding of the infrastructure choices required to support a
   robust client/server environment.
o  Reduction of the time required to choose and design client/server
   infrastructure components.
o  An understanding of the difference between the user's current environment
   and planned, future environment.
o  The Infrastructure Planning Primer, an outline of the steps required to
   plan a client/server infrastructure.
o  Knowledge transfer from Digital consultants to the IS staff.

The ITP service:

o  Decreases the risk of the client/server transition through the use of a
   proven methodology.
o  Reduces the time needed to plan, design, and implement a client/server
   infrastructure.
o  Delivers a proven End-Point Model to users based on open standards.
o  Simplifies a complex planning and design process.
o  Transfers knowledge from Digital consultants to the IS staff.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The BOCSI and ITP services are available now.

BOCSI is offered as an eight- or five-module workshop package with delivery
of a BOCSI report. In the eight-module workshop, the following are
presented:

o  Enterprise Infrastructure Fundamentals
o  Telecommunications for Client/Server Systems
o  Distributed Service Environment -- Core Services
o  Distributed Service Environment -- Extended Services
o  Infrastructure Services For Applications
o  Management of Client/Server Systems
o  Design of Distributed Systems
o  Client/Server Desktop Integration

Users who want the five-module service can select any five modules from the
eight listed based on need and budget; the BOCSI report is also delivered.

