Announcement Highlights

On April 11, Digital is announcing an impressive array of powerful new
client/server products and services. The standard-setting AlphaServer 8400
and 8200 systems significantly enhance the performance of large commercial
and scientific applications running on traditional mainframes but at a
fraction of the cost.

These high-end servers take advantage of the Alpha 64 bit architecture,
coupled with a blazing fast I/O bus, multiple CPUs, and Very Large Memory
capacity. The AlphaServer 8400 5/300 enterprise server supports up to 12
processors, 14 GB of memory, and 1.2 GB per second of I/O. It can deliver
over 3,000 transactions per second, and supports OpenVMS and Digital UNIX
platforms.

The AlphaServer 8200 5/300 server boasts over 2,000 transactions per second,
3.6 gigaflops, and 1.2 GB of I/O per second, offering small enterprises and
large departments the most powerful, expandable, and reliable server at the
best price/performance.

By combining Oracle Corporation's 64-bit Very Large Memory database
capability for commercial UNIX applications with Digital's AlphaServer 8400
and 8200 systems, companies can instantaneously and cost-effectively access
their day-to-day data and expand their computing resources to meet growing
business requirements. With this solution, companies can run large blocks of
their database in main memory, which improves application performance up to
100 times compared to 32-bit systems.

Very Large Memory database technology makes it possible to cache key data
into the expanded memory, reducing the amount of I/O needed, thereby
significantly boosting application performance. Digital is now selling both
ORACLE7 and Oracle Rdb for all AlphaServer systems. Plus, Digital is
discounting software upgrades from Digital Rdb to Oracle Rdb with 90 percent
off list price (minimum $5,000) for organizations moving from a VAX system
to a larger VAX or Alpha system or from an Alpha system to a larger Alpha
system.

The AlphaServer 8200 5/300 is also available as a rackmount server, offering
I/O flexibility usually available only with ultra high end machines by
supporting PCI, XMI, and MVE options. With up to six CPUs, small enterprises
and large departments will have a rackmount enterprise server with
unprecedented performance, capacity, and reliability for those applications
previously available only on large enterprise systems. The AlphaServer 8200
5/300 Rackmount will be available for ordering in April, with shipping to
begin in May. Watch for details in an upcoming issue.

This issue also contains an article on a new financing program being
announced April 11, the LeaseWorks-TurboBridge option. This program offers
aggressive lease rates on the DEC or VAX 7000 system, and provides "bridge
financing" at zero percent interest to finance the upgrade to the new
AlphaServer 8400 system.

Digital also chose to port and release the latest version of IBM's popular
CICS (Customer Information Control System) online transaction processing
monitor with its AlphaServer 8400 and 8200 servers. CICS for Digital UNIX
software enables organizations to port applications to and from many
platforms, including IBM mainframes. By utilizing data communications and
distributed computing industry standards, CICS for Digital UNIX software
running on the AlphaServer 8400 or 8200 systems provides superior
price/performance over CISC software running on other systems.

Also being announced is Digital's new StorageWorks TL810 Automated Tape
Library. Available for the AlphaServer 8400 and 8200 systems, it combines
the economy of tape with the quick access of award-winning Digital Linear
Tape technology. The TL810 adds a less expensive, faster access, automated
tape library to the product family, and is ideal for data center
applications for Digital systems, platforms, clusters, and networks.

Also on April 11, Digital is unveiling its AlphaServer 2000 and 2100 product
lines. These new systems deliver leadership price/performance, supporting as
many as four processors with symmetric multiprocessing. The new AlphaServer
2100 and 2000 systems are optimized to meet user demands for quick response
times, fast application processing, and uninterrupted performance.

The AlphaServer 2000 4/275 is the Alpha 21064A microprocessor at 275 MHz
with a 4 MB secondary cache in the familiar AlphaServer 2000 package. The
AlphaServer 2000 4/233 is the new entry system for the AlphaServer 2000
family, replacing the AlphaServer 2000 4/200 model. It uses the Alpha 21064A
microprocessor at 233 MHz with a 1 MB secondary cache and is binary
compatible with the other members of the AlphServer systems family. This
system provides more performance than its predecessor at the same price.

The AlphaServer 2100 5/250 has the same characteristics as the other
AlphaServer 2100 systems, but provides significantly increased performance
through its use of the new Alpha 21164 microprocessor, at 250 MHz with a 4
MB third level (off chip) cache. The AlphaServer 2100 4/200, 4/233, and
4/275 may be upgraded to the faster processor through a simple, cost
effective processor board swap.

The AlphaServer 2100 4/233 is the new entry system for the AlphaServer 2100
family, replacing the AlphaServer 2100 4/200 model. It uses the Alpha 21064A
microprocessor at 233 MHz with a 1 MB secondary cache. The AlphaServer 2100
Cabinet system has expanded storage capability, allowing a single
AlphaServer 2100 Rackmount system to be configured in a 67-inch high
cabinet, with up to 172 GB of fixed disk storage using 2 GB StorageWorks
drives.

Described in this issue and also being announced on April 11 are additions
to Digital's VAX line -- new VAX 4000, VAXstation 4000, and MicroVAX 3100
systems boost performance by 20 percent. The VAX 4000 Model 106A combines
the power of a 10 ns (nanosecond) NVAX CMOS-4 processor, the richness of
DSSI VMSclustering, the maturity of OpenVMS software, and the ease of Q-bus
connectivity with low-cost memory, power, and packaging.

The MicroVAX 3100 Model 96 is the latest and fastest member of the MicroVAX
family of low-cost, high performance servers. The powerful desktop system
answers users' demands for client/server solutions that combine quality and
performance, at the right price.

The AlphaStation 250 4/266 mid-range performance workstation is ideal for
users who demand high performance and graphics, and require all the benefits
of industry-standard option support. The system supports Digital UNIX,
OpenVMS, and Windows NT operating systems.

Plus, this issue also includes articles on new storage products, including
the StorageWorks BA356-KC wide 16 bit deskside expansion pedestal and fast
wide 16 bit SCSI disks, along with new networking products HUBwatch V4.0 and
the PORTswitch 900 family of per-port switching products. Newly announced
software includes PATHWORKS V5.0 for DEC OSF/1 (NetWare) systems, POLYCENTER
Capacity Planner V3.0, LinkWorks Image, LinkWorks Mass Store, and Multimedia
Services for Digital UNIX V1.5. Be sure to check the table of contents for
details on other related products.



